---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: DaemonSet
metadata:
  name: node-exporter
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: node-exporter
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: node-exporter
        netpol-baseline: enforced
    spec:
      enableServiceLinks: false
      automountServiceAccountToken: false
      # Host networking + PID so node_* describes the NODE (network interfaces, /proc)
      # rather than a pod netns — the standard node-exporter posture. The 9100 listener
      # this opens on each node is host-firewalled by ufw's default-deny INPUT except
      # for the server node's LAN IP (k3s_join_agent_ports): under hostNetwork the
      # "pod IP" prometheus scrapes IS the node's LAN IP, and cross-node that traffic
      # arrives masqueraded from the scraping node — NOT over the vxlan the join allows
      # already covered. (This comment originally claimed the join allows admitted it;
      # wrong — the daniel-server target was down from day one until the 9100 allow
      # landed 2026-08-14.)
      hostNetwork: true
      hostPID: true
      priorityClassName: homelab-best-effort
      containers:
        - name: node-exporter
          image: {{ node_exporter_k8s_image }}
          args:
            - --path.procfs=/host/proc
            - --path.rootfs=/rootfs
            - --path.sysfs=/host/sys
            - --collector.filesystem.mount-points-exclude=^/(sys|proc|dev|host|etc|var/lib/kubelet)($|/)
            - --collector.textfile.directory=/textfile
          ports:
            - containerPort: 9100
          securityContext:
            readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
            allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
            # Host access here is READ-ONLY and comes from the four hostPath mounts below, not
            # from a uid — a 2026-08-31 review assumed the reverse and reported this as needing
            # root. It measures 65534 on both nodes from the image's own USER, so pinning it
            # changes nothing today and guards the image bump that would.
            runAsNonRoot: true
            runAsUser: 65534
            capabilities:
              drop:
                - ALL
          # PROBING /metrics COSTS ONE LOG LINE PER UNWRITTEN METRIC FAMILY, and until
          # 2026-08-29 both probes did it. kubelet's HTTP prober reads a bounded prefix of the
          # response and closes; /metrics is 250,165 bytes over 312 families (measured on
          # daniel-box), so every probe was cut off mid-body and node_exporter logged
          #
          #     error encoding and sending metric family: write tcp ...: connection reset by peer
          #
          # once for each family it could no longer write — ~180 lines per probe. At 6 readiness
          # + 2 liveness probes a minute that was 21-24 lines/sec/pod, and 97% of ALL
          # k8s-namespace Loki ingest.
          #
          # It is NOT a throttling problem, though it looked like one: node-exporter was
          # throttled on 42% of CFS periods, and raising the CPU limit to 1 core took throttling
          # to zero while the flood stayed at 21-24 lines/sec. The prober closes after its fixed
          # read no matter how fast the body is produced.
          #
          # Neither is it fixable by making the response smaller. node_exporter serves exactly
          # two 200 paths — there is no /-/healthy (404, checked) — and `?collect[]=<one
          # collector>` still costs ~10KB because the go_*/process_*/promhttp_* floor rides
          # along on every filtered response; that lands within ~200 bytes of the prober's read
          # limit, a margin an image bump could erase silently, back into this same flood. A
          # bogus collector name is small but answers 400.
          livenessProbe:
            httpGet:
              path: /metrics
              port: 9100
            initialDelaySeconds: 15
            # 300s, not 30s. This is the one probe that still renders metrics, because it is the
            # only thing that restarts a container whose collector has WEDGED — a hung collector
            # (a dead NFS mount is the classic) leaves the listener answering / while /metrics
            # hangs, and only a probe that asks for metrics times out on it. Keeping that at 30s
            # would keep 360 lines/min of the flood for a fault that has never been seen here, so
            # it is kept and slowed rather than dropped: ~36 lines/min, a 97.5% cut.
            # The cost is recovery latency, not detection latency — 3 failures at 300s is up to
            # 15 min to restart, while Prometheus scrapes at 1m and monitor-bridge's Scrape
            # Targets check pages on up{job="node"} == 0 within its 5-min cycle regardless.
            periodSeconds: 300
            # /metrics measured at 0.30s (daniel-box) / 0.34s (daniel-server); 3s keeps headroom
            # over the 1s default, since scrape cost grows with mount/disk count.
            timeoutSeconds: 3
          # `rollout status daemonset/node-exporter` gates on numberAvailable (== numberReady,
          # since minReadySeconds is unset), which without a readiness probe means "Running" —
          # it would return green on a container that never served a scrape. Shorter delay than
          # the liveness probe: this one decides when the rollout is allowed to proceed, not
          # when to kill the container.
          #
          # `/` (1,490 bytes, HTTP 200) rather than /metrics, and it gates the rollout just as
          # well: node_exporter serves both paths from ONE listener that binds after the
          # collectors register, so a container that could not serve a scrape cannot serve this
          # either. What / does not catch is the wedged-collector case above, which is why
          # liveness still asks for metrics. Note the probe never distinguished a healthy
          # exporter from one whose collectors all fail in the first place — node_exporter
          # answers 200 either way and the prober discards the body, so that half of the
          # original rationale was never delivered by the path it justified.
          readinessProbe:
            httpGet:
              path: /
              port: 9100
            initialDelaySeconds: 5
            periodSeconds: 10
            timeoutSeconds: 3
          volumeMounts:
            - name: node-exporter-proc
              mountPath: /host/proc
              readOnly: true
            - name: node-exporter-sys
              mountPath: /host/sys
              readOnly: true
            - name: node-exporter-rootfs
              mountPath: /rootfs
              readOnly: true
            - name: node-exporter-textfile
              mountPath: /textfile
              readOnly: true
          resources:
            requests:
              cpu: 20m
              memory: 32Mi
            limits:
              # 1 core, not 200m. This exporter renders ~320 metric families per request and was
              # throttled on 42.4% of CFS periods (daniel-box) / 31.7% (daniel-server), measured
              # 2026-08-29. On its own that ratio is close to an artifact — absolute throttled
              # time is ~1.6% of wall-clock — but here it has a second, real consequence: the
              # kubelet's own liveness and readiness probes GET /metrics and close after reading a
              # bounded prefix, and a slower render leaves more of the response unwritten when
              # they do. node_exporter then logs one "connection reset by peer" line per unwritten
              # family. Hit rate tracks throttle: 94% of probe ticks produce a burst on daniel-box
              # against 41% on daniel-server. That flood was 97% of all k8s-namespace Loki ingest.
              # Raising the ceiling shrinks it; the probe path is the actual cause and is
              # unchanged here.
              cpu: "1"
              memory: 128Mi
      volumes:
        - name: node-exporter-proc
          hostPath:
            path: /proc
        - name: node-exporter-sys
          hostPath:
            path: /sys
        - name: node-exporter-rootfs
          hostPath:
            path: /
        # Host cron scripts may drop *.prom gauges here (the kopia-era B2-usage pattern);
        # empty today, kept as the hook so a future host gauge needs no exporter change.
        - name: node-exporter-textfile
          hostPath:
            path: /var/lib/node-exporter-textfile
            type: DirectoryOrCreate
